exec(open(__file__.rsplit("/",1)[0] + "/_h.py").read())
B("Job", """
1|Job is a blameless, wealthy man who fears God. Satan says he only serves God because he is blessed. God allows Satan to take Job's children and possessions, and Job says: the Lord gave, the Lord has taken away, blessed be the name of the Lord.
2|Satan strikes Job with painful sores. Job's wife tells him to curse God and die, but he refuses. Three friends, Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar, come and sit silently with him for seven days.
3|Job curses the day he was born and wonders why people who suffer are kept alive.
4|Eliphaz says that no innocent person has ever perished, and suggests Job must have sinned. He describes a vision in which he heard a voice say no one is pure before God.
5|Eliphaz tells Job to seek God. He says God rescues the lowly, and happy is the person God corrects.
6|Job says his suffering is heavier than the sand of the sea and he longs for death. He says his friends have failed him like a dried-up stream.
7|Job says life is hard labor and his days are empty. He asks God why he treats him as an enemy and asks God to leave him alone.
8|Bildad says God does not twist justice. He tells Job that if he is pure, God will restore him.
9|Job says no one can be right before God, who is too powerful. He wishes for someone to stand between God and himself.
10|Job tells God he is weary of life and asks why God made him only to destroy him. He asks for a little comfort before he dies.
11|Zophar says Job's words are empty and tells him to put away sin. If he turns to God, his life will be brighter than noon.
12|Job says he knows as much as his friends do, and that wisdom and power belong to God, who controls nations and rulers.
13|Job says his friends are speaking falsely for God. He says he will argue his case to God directly: though he slay me, I will hope in him.
14|Job says that life is short and full of trouble. A tree that is cut down may sprout again, but a person who dies does not rise until the heavens are no more.
15|Eliphaz accuses Job of speaking foolishly. He says the wicked suffer all their days.
16|Job calls his friends miserable comforters. He says God has worn him out, but he believes his witness is in heaven.
17|Job's spirit is broken and the grave is waiting. He says mockers surround him, and asks who will be his hope.
18|Bildad describes the dreadful end of the wicked and says Job is like one of them.
19|Job says his friends torment him. He says: I know that my Redeemer lives, and in my flesh I shall see God.
20|Zophar says the wicked enjoy their triumph only briefly and that God will bring them to ruin.
21|Job answers that the wicked often live long and prosper. He says his friends' comfort is nonsense.
22|Eliphaz accuses Job of specific sins, such as not helping the poor, and tells him to make peace with God.
23|Job longs to find God and present his case. He says that when God has tested him, he will come out as gold.
24|Job asks why God does not set times for judgment. He lists the injustices of the wicked, who oppress the poor.
25|Bildad says that God is great and no human being can be pure before him.
26|Job says God's power stretches over the heavens and the deep: he hangs the earth on nothing. These are only the edges of his ways.
27|Job vows he will not say anything false and will keep his integrity as long as he lives. He describes the fate of the wicked.
28|Where can wisdom be found? Humans can dig for silver and gold, but wisdom cannot be bought. The fear of the Lord, that is wisdom.
29|Job remembers his earlier life, when God watched over him, people honored him and he helped the poor.
30|Now young men mock Job. He is in pain and says God does not answer him.
31|Job swears he is innocent of lust, dishonesty and mistreating the poor or his servants, and asks to be heard by God.
32|Elihu, a younger man, speaks up. He is angry at Job for declaring himself right and at the three friends for failing to answer him.
33|Elihu says that God speaks to people through dreams and through pain, in order to keep them from the pit.
34|Elihu says God does not act wickedly or pervert justice. He sees everyone and no one hides from him.
35|Elihu says that Job is wrong to say there is no benefit in doing right. He says God is not affected by our sin or goodness in the same way we are.
36|Elihu says that God uses suffering to teach people. He describes God's greatness in creation.
37|Elihu describes God's voice in the thunder and the weather, and says that the Almighty is beyond our reach.
38|God answers Job out of the whirlwind. Where were you when I laid the foundations of the earth? God asks Job about the sea, the dawn, the snow and the stars.
39|God continues to question Job about wild animals: mountain goats, the wild donkey, the ostrich, the horse and the hawk. God cares for them all.
40|God tells Job to answer him. Job says he is unworthy and puts his hand over his mouth. God describes the great beast Behemoth.
41|God describes Leviathan, a sea creature no one can tame, as an example of his power.
42|Job answers: I had heard of you by the hearing of the ear, but now my eye sees you. I repent in dust and ashes. God says that the friends have not spoken rightly about him, and Job prays for them. God restores Job's fortunes and doubles what he had.
""")
B("Proverbs", """
1|Solomon's proverbs begin by saying their purpose is to teach wisdom. The fear of the Lord is the beginning of knowledge. A father warns his son not to follow sinners.
2|Wisdom is like treasure to be searched for. If you seek it, the Lord gives wisdom and protects you from evil paths.
3|Trust in the Lord with all your heart and do not rely on your own understanding. Honor the Lord with your wealth, and accept his discipline.
4|A father passes on what he was taught: get wisdom, guard your heart, and keep your feet from evil.
5|A father warns his son against adultery and urges him to be faithful to the wife of his youth.
6|The chapter warns against pledging money for someone else's debts, laziness, a lying tongue, and adultery. It lists seven things the Lord hates.
7|A father describes a young man led astray by an adulterous woman, and warns that her house is the way to death.
8|Wisdom calls out from the heights and the crossroads. She says that the Lord made her at the beginning, before the earth, and she was beside him as a craftsman.
9|Wisdom prepares a feast and invites the simple to eat. Folly is a loud woman who calls to people passing by, and those who follow her end up in the grave.
10|This begins the main collection of Solomon's proverbs. They contrast the righteous and the wicked, and the wise and the foolish. A wise son makes a glad father.
11|Honest scales please the Lord. Pride leads to disgrace, but humility brings wisdom. A generous person will prosper.
12|Whoever loves discipline loves knowledge. A good person obtains favor from the Lord. The hand of the diligent will rule.
13|A wise child listens to a father's instruction. Hope deferred makes the heart sick, but a longing fulfilled is a tree of life.
14|A wise woman builds her house. There is a way that seems right to a person, but its end is the way of death.
15|A soft answer turns away wrath, but a harsh word stirs up anger. The eyes of the Lord are in every place.
16|The plans of the heart belong to humans, but the answer of the tongue is from the Lord. Pride goes before destruction.
17|A friend loves at all times. A cheerful heart is good medicine. Better a dry crust with peace than a house full of feasting and quarreling.
18|The name of the Lord is a strong tower, and the righteous run into it and are safe. Death and life are in the power of the tongue.
19|It is better to be poor and honest than to be a fool and lie. Whoever is kind to the poor lends to the Lord. The Lord's purpose will stand.
20|Wine is a mocker. Honest weights are the Lord's delight. Even a child is known by his actions.
21|The king's heart is in the Lord's hand, like a stream of water. To do righteousness and justice is more acceptable than sacrifice.
22|A good name is better than great riches. Train up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old he will not depart from it.
23|The chapter warns against greed, drunkenness and gluttony. Do not wear yourself out to get rich. Buy the truth and do not sell it.
24|Do not envy the wicked. A righteous person falls seven times and rises again. Do not gloat when your enemy falls.
25|These are more proverbs of Solomon, copied by the men of King Hezekiah. If your enemy is hungry, give him food to eat, and if he is thirsty, give him water.
26|Several proverbs describe fools and lazy people. As a dog returns to its vomit, so a fool repeats his folly.
27|Do not boast about tomorrow, for you do not know what a day may bring. As iron sharpens iron, so one person sharpens another.
28|The wicked flee when no one pursues, but the righteous are bold as a lion. Whoever conceals his sins does not prosper, but the one who confesses and forsakes them finds mercy.
29|Where there is no vision the people perish, but blessed is the one who keeps the law. A person who stiffens his neck after many rebukes will suddenly be broken.
30|The sayings of Agur include a prayer: give me neither poverty nor riches, but only my daily bread. It also lists things that are wonderful or too hard to understand.
31|King Lemuel's mother teaches him to be just and defend the poor. The book ends with a poem about a woman of strength and good character, whose children call her blessed.
""")
B("Ecclesiastes", """
1|The Teacher says: vanity of vanities, all is vanity. Generations come and go, and nothing is new under the sun. Wisdom brings sorrow.
2|The Teacher tries pleasure, wine, great projects and riches, and finds it is all vanity. Wisdom is better than folly, but the wise and the fool both die.
3|For everything there is a season: a time to be born and a time to die, a time to weep and a time to laugh. God has set eternity in human hearts.
4|The Teacher sees oppression, and envy in work. Two are better than one, and a cord of three strands is not quickly broken.
5|Guard your steps when you go to the house of God. Let your words be few. Those who love money never have enough.
6|Wealth without the ability to enjoy it is vanity. Desires are never satisfied, and no one knows what is good for a person in this short life.
7|A good name is better than precious oil. It is better to go to a house of mourning than to a house of feasting. Wisdom is a shelter.
8|Wisdom makes a face shine. Obey the king. The wicked sometimes seem to prosper, but it will go well with those who fear God.
9|The same fate comes to everyone. Whatever your hand finds to do, do it with your might. Time and chance happen to all.
10|A little folly outweighs wisdom. A fool's lips are his ruin. The chapter has many sayings about rulers and work.
11|Cast your bread upon the waters, for you will find it after many days. Rejoice, young person, in your youth, but know that God will bring you to judgment.
12|Remember your Creator in the days of your youth, before the days of trouble come. The Teacher ends: fear God and keep his commandments, for this is the whole duty of everyone.
""")
B("Song of Songs", """
1|The bride and her beloved speak of their love. She says: let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth. She is dark and lovely, and asks where he feeds his flock.
2|The beloved compares her to a lily among thorns. She says: I am my beloved's and he is mine. Winter is past, and the time of singing has come.
3|The bride looks for her beloved at night and finds him. A royal procession is described, with Solomon's carriage.
4|The beloved praises her beauty, calling her a garden locked up. She invites him to come into his garden.
5|The beloved knocks at her door at night, but she is slow to answer and he is gone. She searches for him and describes him to the women of Jerusalem.
6|The women ask where he has gone. She says: I am my beloved's, and my beloved is mine. He says she is beautiful, like an army with banners.
7|The beloved admires her, from her feet to her head. She invites him to go out to the fields together.
8|Set me as a seal upon your heart. Love is strong as death, and many waters cannot quench it. Her brothers speak, and the book ends with her calling her beloved to come away quickly.
""")
B("Wisdom", """
1|Rulers are told to love righteousness and seek the Lord with a sincere heart. Wisdom will not enter a deceitful soul. God did not make death.
2|The wicked say that life is short and we should enjoy it. They plan to oppress the righteous. The writer says God made humans for immortality.
3|The souls of the righteous are in the hand of God, and no torment will touch them. They seem to die, but they are at peace. The wicked will be punished.
4|It is better to be childless and virtuous than to have wicked children. The righteous person who dies young is taken by God.
5|At the judgment the wicked will see the righteous standing with confidence. They will regret their choices. The righteous live forever and God will protect them.
6|Kings and rulers are warned to rule justly. Wisdom is easily found by those who seek her, and the beginning of wisdom is the desire for instruction.
7|Solomon says he prayed and wisdom came. He describes wisdom as a breath of God's power and a spotless mirror of God's work.
8|Solomon says he loved wisdom from his youth and wanted to marry her. Wisdom teaches self-control, prudence, justice and courage.
9|Solomon's prayer: God of my ancestors, give me wisdom, which sits by your throne. Who can know your will unless you give wisdom?
10|Wisdom guided the righteous through history, from Adam to Noah, Abraham, Lot, Jacob, Joseph and Moses, and led Israel out of Egypt.
11|The writer contrasts what God did for Israel with the plagues on Egypt. The same water that was the Egyptians' punishment was the Israelites' drink in the desert. God is merciful to all.
12|God punished the old inhabitants of Canaan gradually, giving them time to repent. He is the Lord of all, so he can be merciful. The righteous must be kind.
13|People who worship nature, or idols they have made, are foolish. They should instead know the one who made all these things.
14|The writer speaks against making idols, and shows how idolatry began and led to injustice and corruption. He praises the wood of the ark.
15|The writer says Israel knows God and does not worship idols. The idol maker is a fool, and gave life to a clay figure.
16|God punishes the Egyptians with animals but feeds Israel with quail and manna, which tasted as each person wished.
17|The plague of darkness fell on Egypt. The wicked were prisoners of their fear in the dark night.
18|Israel had light during the plague of darkness. God took the firstborn of Egypt and saved Israel, and Aaron stood between the living and the dead.
19|Egypt pursues Israel and is drowned in the Red Sea. The sea became a road for Israel, and creation itself served God's people.
""")
B("Sirach", """
1|All wisdom comes from the Lord. The fear of the Lord is the beginning, the fullness, the crown and the root of wisdom.
2|My child, if you come to serve the Lord, prepare yourself for testing. Trust in him, as gold is tried in fire.
3|Honor your father and mother, and God will honor you. Be humble. Do not look into things that are too hard for you.
4|Do not refuse help to the poor. Be like a father to orphans. Wisdom teaches her children and takes care of those who seek her.
5|Do not rely on your wealth or presume on God's mercy. Be quick to listen and slow to answer.
6|A faithful friend is a sturdy shelter. A person who finds one finds a treasure. Come to wisdom and she will give you rest.
7|Do not do evil and evil will not overtake you. Be humble, honor the priest, and care for the poor and the sick.
8|The writer advises about dealing with people: do not quarrel with the powerful, do not despise old people, and do not trust strangers too quickly.
9|Do not be jealous of your wife or give a woman power over you. Avoid women who tempt you, and choose friends and counselors carefully.
10|A wise judge instructs his people. Pride is hateful to God and people. The fear of the Lord is the glory of rich and poor alike.
11|Do not praise someone's looks or condemn someone's appearance. Do not envy a sinner's success. God can change a person's situation quickly.
12|Be careful whom you help. A wicked person who has been helped will turn against the helper. An enemy cannot be trusted.
13|Whoever touches pitch will be stained. Do not associate with people more powerful than you. The wealthy and the poor are treated differently.
14|Happy is the one whose conscience does not condemn him. Use your wealth well, because death will come.
15|Whoever fears the Lord does what is right. God has set before you life and death, and he has given everyone the freedom to choose.
16|Do not rejoice in many wicked children. God's judgment is certain, and he is not hidden from anyone. His mercy and wrath are both real.
17|God made humans from earth and gave them strength, intelligence, speech and dominion. Return to the Lord and turn away from sin.
18|The Lord is merciful. Think before you speak, and do not be proud when you do a kindness.
19|Do not be a slave to wine or desire. Be careful what you say about others. True wisdom is not cleverness.
20|There is a time to be silent and a time to speak. A fool talks too much. A lie is an ugly thing in the mouth of a person.
21|Sin is like a double-edged sword. A fool laughs loudly, but a wise person smiles quietly. A fool's heart is in his mouth, but a wise person's mouth is in his heart.
22|The writer speaks of lazy people, foolish children and fools. A friend is faithful when someone is poor, but not when they are betrayed.
23|The writer prays that God will keep him from sins of the tongue and of the flesh. He warns against swearing, and against adultery.
24|Wisdom praises herself in the assembly: I came forth from the mouth of the Most High. She says she made her dwelling in Israel, and is identified with the law of Moses.
25|The writer lists what pleases him and what he hates. He praises the one who finds wisdom and fears the Lord, and warns against a bad wife.
26|A good wife is a blessing, and her husband is happy. A silent, modest wife is a gift. The writer warns about unfaithful women.
27|Those who seek riches tend to do wrong. The writer says a person's speech shows his character. Whoever holds a grudge will find that it comes back on him.
28|Forgive your neighbor's wrong and then your own sins will be pardoned when you pray. Anger and wrath are abominable. Watch your tongue.
29|Lend to your neighbor in his need and pay back on time. Give alms, because it will rescue you from every disaster. Be content with little.
30|Discipline your children. A healthy body is better than great wealth. Do not worry yourself with sorrow, but be cheerful.
31|Wakefulness over riches wastes the flesh. Blessed is the rich person who is found blameless. Be moderate at a banquet.
32|When you are a host at a feast, take care of the guests and then sit down. Honor the Lord with your gifts and trust his law.
33|No evil will come to the one who fears the Lord. God made people different. Do not give your authority to anyone else while you live.
34|Dreams are vain for fools. A person who is traveled is wise. Offerings made from unjust gain are not accepted by God.
35|Keeping the law is like making an offering. Give to God generously. The prayer of the poor pierces the clouds.
36|A prayer for Israel: have mercy on us, O Lord, and gather all the tribes of Jacob. The writer also gives advice on choosing a wife.
37|Be careful whom you take as a friend. Be wary of counselors who seek their own advantage. Listen to your own heart, too, and pray to the Most High.
38|Honor the physician, whom God created. Pray, but also use medicine. The writer says weeping for the dead should be brief, and notes that farmers and craftsmen do not have time to be scholars.
39|The scribe who studies the law of the Most High gains wisdom. The writer praises God's works in a hymn: all the works of the Lord are very good.
40|The writer describes the toil and troubles every person has from birth. He says that a good wife and wisdom are better than gold, and that the fear of the Lord is like a garden of blessings.
41|O death, how bitter is the thought of you to someone who is content with his possessions. The writer lists things to be ashamed of and tells people to keep a good name.
42|The writer lists things you should not be ashamed of, and describes the daughter who worries her father. The second part begins to praise God's works.
43|A hymn of praise to the Lord in creation. The sun, moon, rainbow, snow, wind, sea and stars all reflect his glory. We cannot fully speak his praise.
44|Let us now praise famous men. The writer begins a long section praising the great ancestors, such as Enoch, Noah and Abraham.
45|The writer praises Moses and Aaron, and the priesthood given to Aaron and Phinehas.
46|The writer praises Joshua, Caleb, the judges and the prophet Samuel.
47|The writer praises Nathan, David and Solomon. He says Solomon's wisdom was great but that he foolishly gave himself over to women.
48|The writer praises Elijah, Elisha, Hezekiah and Isaiah.
49|The writer praises Josiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel and the twelve prophets, and the rebuilders Zerubbabel, Jeshua and Nehemiah.
50|The writer praises the high priest Simon, son of Onias, who restored the temple. He describes him coming out of the sanctuary like the morning star, and ends with a blessing.
51|The author gives thanks to God for saving him. He says he sought wisdom from his youth and found her, and invites the uneducated to come and learn.
""")
